1: # The literal primitive-scalar C1 native arrow
2: 
3: Unpromoted consistency calculation, 17 November 2026. This note
4: uses a concrete primitive scalar family of P3K.22 (for example
5: P3K.10's OS45 family), not the source-zero family of P3K.60.
6: It records the complete local arrow which must be compared with
7: P3K.25's characteristic bordism and P3K.29's initial product.
8: No earlier proved status is changed by this working note.
9: 
10: ## SCN.1. The actual primitive scalar seam
11: 
12: Keep the physical line character \(V\) and the original positive
13: \(\lambda\)-loop of K22/K25. The full periods are
14: \[
15:  h:(Z,V,q)\mapsto(\kappa(q)qZ,\alpha(q)V,q),\qquad
16:  B:(Z,V,q)\mapsto(g(q)Z,q\lambda\beta(q)V,q),
17: \]
18: where all displayed units are independent of the scalar parameter.
19: Put \(\eta=\lambda\beta\). The primitive cap-to-ordinary gluing of
20: K10/K22 has the literal forward parameter arrow
21: \[
22:                   R_{\rm ord}:(Z,V,q)\mapsto(Z,q^{-1}V,q).
23:                                                         \tag{SCN.1}
24: \]
25: Its cap arrow is the holomorphic unit recorded in K10; the exact
26: overlap square there is retained unchanged. Reindexing the shifted
27: cone by the actual period \(B\) gives
28: \[
29:                     E_\lambda:(Z,V)\mapsto(gZ,\eta V).
30:                                                         \tag{SCN.2}
31: \]
32: This is the K25.6 endpoint in global torus coordinates; in its
33: local square coordinates \((Z_{\rm loc},V_{\rm loc})=(Z,ZV)\)
34: it is \((gZ_{\rm loc},g\eta V_{\rm loc})\).
35: 
36: On a cut scalar interval \(\tau\in[0,1]\), let
37: \(\lambda=\lambda_*e^{2\pi i\tau}\). Retain the actual logarithms
38: of \(g,\eta\) on the \(q\)-disc times this interval, and take a
39: cutoff \(\chi=0\) near the first end, \(\chi=1\) near the last.
40: Use
41: \[
42:  C^{(1)}_\tau:(Z,V)\mapsto(AZ,DV),\qquad
43:  A=e^{-\chi\log g},\quad D=e^{-\chi\log\eta}.         \tag{SCN.3}
44: \]
45: On the terminal collar \(C^{(1)}=E_\lambda^{-1}\), so the
46: entire seam identity \(C_{\rm end}^{(1)}E_\lambda=1\) holds
47: with every derivative. These maps preserve \(q\), commute with
48: both periods and the physical circle, and have the two actual
49: chart expressions
50: \[
51:  (a,b,c)\mapsto((AD)^{-1}a,Ab,Dc),\qquad
52:  (u,v,w)\mapsto(ADu,D^{-1}v,A^{-1}w).
53:                                                         \tag{SCN.4}
54: \]
55: Substitution in each of DZ.8's three transition rows preserves
56: every coefficient. Thus these are actual closed vertex charts,
57: with the original units and forward \(q^{-1}\) arrow.
58: 
59: The doubled seam is specified as the literal pullback
60: \[
61:  C^{(2)}_{j+\tau}=C^{(1)}_\tau E_{\lambda(\tau)}^{-j},
62:                        \qquad j=0,1.                \tag{SCN.5}
63: \]
64: Its middle and terminal collar identities are respectively
65: \(E^{-1}\) and \(E^{-2}\). It has not been independently chosen
66: from the doubled endpoint.
67: 
68: ## SCN.2. The complete native C1 product and Cox lift
69: 
70: In these closed charts the horizontal native star is given by
71: \[
72:  (z,n_0,n_1)=(c,a,b),\qquad
73:  (z',n'_0,n'_1)=(\eta v,g w,(g\eta)^{-1}u).
74:                                                         \tag{SCN.6}
75: \]
76: The full \(C_1\) transition row is
77: \[
78:               (u,v,w)=(g\eta bc,\eta^{-1}c^{-1},g^{-1}ac).
79: \]
80: Hence (SCN.6) satisfies exactly
81: \[
82:                          z'=z^{-1},\qquad n'_i=zn_i.
83:                                                         \tag{SCN.7}
84: \]
85: This is a single native star product with all parameter units
86: included. On its first chart (SCN.4) acts by
87: \[
88:                  (z,n_0,n_1)\mapsto
89:                     (Dz,(AD)^{-1}n_0,An_1).
90:                                                         \tag{SCN.8}
91: \]
92: On the second it is
93: \((z',n'_0,n'_1)\mapsto(D^{-1}z',A^{-1}n'_0,ADn'_1)\);
94: the equality (SCN.7) is retained literally.
95: 
96: For Cox coordinates with auxiliary weights \((1,1,-1,-1)\),
97: put \(z=a_1/a_0,n_i=a_0c_i\). The lift of (SCN.8) is
98: \[
99:  (a_0,a_1,c_0,c_1)\mapsto
100:        (a_0,D a_1,(AD)^{-1}c_0,Ac_1).                \tag{SCN.9}
101: \]
102: It preserves \(q=a_0a_1c_0c_1\), so its inverse has the same
103: \(q\) and inverse multipliers even for the full \(q\)-dependent
104: units. It extends through both small resolutions. In this order
105: the physical circle weights are \((0,1,-1,0)\). The angular
106: factor of (SCN.9) is the corresponding physical-circle factor;
107: the inverse direction from K25.7 is retained by (SCN.3).
108: The native product pulled back under the double parameter cover
109: is exactly the one using (SCN.5), including (SCN.6).
110: 
111: ## SCN.3. The doubled ordinary normal maps and trace degrees
112: 
113: The raw period-unit winding is
114: \(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&0\\0&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)\)
115: for one positive primitive loop. For two turns, with
116: \(\lambda=\lambda_*e^{2i\theta}\), the actual based normal
117: ratios in the second-vertex product
118: \((u,\eta v,\kappa w)\) are exactly
119: \[
120:  K_0=K_2=\operatorname{diag}(e^{2i\theta},1),
121:  \qquad K_1=\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{2i\theta}).       \tag{SCN.10}
122: \]
123: All other units cancel against their identical reference values.
124: The original higher normal terms are carried by the same
125: normal-rescaling and cutoff-flow construction as DZ.2. Thus the
126: closed ordinary product uses
127: \[
128:  t=1,\qquad l=(2,0,2),\qquad
129:  T_i(Q)=H_1 QH_{l_i-1}.
130: \]
131: The physical weights remain \(w=(0,1,-1)\),
132: \(d=(-2,-1,-1)\). Substitution in the literal orbit map DZ.16
133: and its closed stable derivative gives
134: \[
135:                   I=(-2,2,-2),\qquad W=(2,0,0).
136:                                                         \tag{SCN.11}
137: \]
138: The actual capped graph-loop prism and the ambient-frame
139: comparison then give on \(C_1\)
140: \[
141:  V_{11}=2,\qquad\delta_1=-2,\qquad
142:        2n_1=\delta_1-V_{11}=-4,\qquad m_1=0.
143:                                                         \tag{SCN.12}
144: \]
145: The native-to-ordinary boundary arrow in this star is
146: \[
147:                     q_H\mapsto
148:                    (U H_1U^{-1})q_H H_{-1}.
149:                                                         \tag{SCN.13}
150: \]
151: Its Hopf-oriented left degree is \(+2\), and its right degree
152: is zero. Extending its inverse by OR.2's actual cylinder map
153: gives the pure-left endpoint \(L^{-2}\). The doubled native
154: pair obtained from (SCN.12) is therefore \((2,-2)\).
155: 
156: The product (SCN.5)--(SCN.7) is the literal pullback of the
157: single native product, so the same fixed-boundary connection
158: argument of DZ.4 identifies its relative return with \(f^2\).
159: Its two free coordinates consequently give the single native
160: pair
161: \[
162:                                (m(f),n(f))=(1,-1).
163:                                                         \tag{SCN.14}
164: \]
165: This uses the free coordinates of the actual relative square;
166: it does not divide a seven-sphere class.
167: 
168: ## SCN.4. The exact comparison still to be audited
169: 
170: The local square in K25 is the horizontal star of (SCN.6):
171: its original edge joins \((0,0)\) to \((1,0)\), and its other
172: vertices are \((1,-1),(0,1)\). Its characters are precisely
173: \((Z_{\rm loc},V_{\rm loc})=(Z,ZV)\), as in SCN.1.
174: Thus (SCN.2), (SCN.8) and (SCN.9) provide the full native
175: closing dictionary for the stated K25 parameter family.
176: 
177: The relative Cox conjugation used in K23 would attach central
178: change \(-1\) to (SCN.14), whereas the K25 characteristic
179: bordism records zero. The map or relative-boundary term
180: responsible for this difference has not yet been identified.
181: The two concrete derivations are preserved for root audit.
182: In particular, K29's passage from K27's fixed-star cusp
183: product to a delta-equivariant product of the entire
184: three-curve union must be checked as an actual map; the
185: fixed-star normal product alone is not that missing map.
186: 
187: This working note is not exported as a theorem. It does not
188: alter the exact source-zero \(C_2\) comparison of K60, whose
189: same scalar test gives zero \(C_2\) right coordinate.
